+    // Forward branch. The distance is not known yet, but it does not have to
+    // be: emit the single bc and let bind_to() deal with it. If the label
+    // turns out to be further than bc's 16-bit reach, bind_to() allocates a
+    // trampoline slot and reroutes the fixup through it -- it already computes
+    // max_reach_from() per fixup and max_reach_from() already returns 16 for
+    // BCX. That path simply was never reachable, because this function
+    // previously emitted the two-instruction form for *every* forward branch
+    // regardless of how near the target turned out to be.
+    //
+    // Measured on TurboFan output for crypto+richards+deltablue: 4589 of 4988
+    // conditional branches (92%) were the two-instruction pair, i.e. ~7% of
+    // all generated instructions existed only to work around not knowing the
+    // distance yet. It also doubles the branch footprint seen by the
+    // predictor, since each forward conditional became a not-taken bc plus an
+    // unconditional b.
+    //
+    // The escape hatch only exists while a trampoline pool can still be
+    // emitted. CheckTrampolinePool() emits exactly one pool per code object
+    // and then sets trampoline_emitted_, after which an out-of-reach fixup has
+    // nowhere to go and bind_to()'s get_trampoline_entry() would fail. Large
+    // generated bodies -- Irregexp in particular -- keep emitting branches
+    // long after that point, so once the net is gone fall back to the
+    // two-instruction form, which always reaches.
+    if (trampoline_emitted_) {
+      Label skip;
+      bc_short(NegateCondition(cond), &skip, cr);
+      b(L, lk);
+      bind(&skip);
+      return;
+    }
+    bc_short(cond, L, cr, lk);
